abstract: >-
  PHOTO-CAT is a catalog-driven, mission-agnostic tool for quantifying stellar flux
  contamination in wide-field photometric observations. High-precision photometric
  missions are sensitive to flux from neighboring stars, which can bias light curves,
  dilute transit depths, and degrade photometric precision; PHOTO-CAT screens targets
  for that risk directly from an astrometric catalog, before an observation is planned.

  The pipeline builds a three-dimensional Euclidean representation of the sky from a
  user-provided catalog and indexes it with a memory-optimized KDTree for rapid
  neighbor searches. A streaming, CSR-style storage format with compact internal
  identifiers keeps memory overhead low enough to process catalogs with tens to
  hundreds of millions of sources. Per-target contamination is then computed from
  angular separations, relative fluxes, and a configurable aperture model: a top-hat,
  or a circular Gaussian PSF that weights each neighbor by its radial flux decay out
  to a PSF-derived influence radius.

  Catalog flux can optionally be converted into another photometric band before
  contamination is computed, either by integrating a blackbody spectrum through the
  band's transmission curve, which works for any mission passband including filters
  pulled from the SVO Filter Profile Service, or by applying a published Gaia
  empirical colour transformation, for the photometric systems and colour ranges those
  relations are calibrated on. Official transmission curves for Gaia, TESS, CHEOPS,
  MAUVE and the six Ariel channels ship with the package.

  Outputs include per-target contaminating flux, contamination ratios, target purity,
  the number of neighboring sources and their angular separations, written as JSON
  alongside reproducibility metadata recording the package version, the query
  configuration, the index manifest and the explicit scope of the model. The tool runs
  from a graphical configurator or from a command-line interface. Results are
  catalog-level screening estimates, meant to support target selection and
  field-geometry decisions ahead of observations rather than to correct measured
  photometry.

  The method and its application to the Ariel Mission Candidate Sample are described
  in G. Galletta et al. (2026), "PHOTO-CAT - Photometric Contamination Analyzer Tool:
  A High-Performance Framework for Photometric Contamination Assessment"; this
  reference is updated with its DOI once the article is published. Although developed
  for the Mauve satellite, PHOTO-CAT is mission-agnostic and readily applicable to
  TESS, PLATO, Ariel and other wide-field surveys.
